@@ -341,6 +341,35 @@ Playback Control
     Ordinary seek/pause commands and file unload cancel transport-owned fades.
     Outputs without PCM fading support use ordinary transport without fading.
 
+``crossfade-loadfile <url> <duration> [linear|equal-power] [<options>]``
+    Replace playback with an opt-in PCM cross-fade to another file. ``duration``
+    is from 0 to 30 seconds. The command retains upcoming, unplayed PCM from the
+    outgoing file and mixes it with the incoming decoder's output on the same
+    audio device. It never replays audio that has already been heard.
+
+    Configure ``--audio-crossfade-buffer`` before playback to reserve enough
+    outgoing PCM. Without sufficient buffering, the overlap is shorter than
+    requested. The result map includes ``playlist_entry_id`` and ``captured``
+    (retained seconds). ``audio-crossfade`` reports live output state. Memory is
+    bounded to 64 MiB each for the optional lookahead and the outgoing reserve.
+
+    Linear is the default curve; equal-power is also available and may require
+    extra peak headroom. Per-file ReplayGain is retained separately for each
+    source. User volume, mute, and the ordinary fade envelope affect both sources
+    together. Pausing freezes the overlap; seeking or stopping discards it.
+    Incoming PCM is converted to the retained device's rate, format and channels.
+    A known short incoming duration limits the overlap to avoid an outgoing tail
+    continuing after the incoming file has ended.
+
+    The outgoing audio fades while the incoming file opens. If the incoming
+    decoder is late, the overlap is shortened; if it is not ready before the
+    reserve ends, playback falls back to ordinary loading. This is best-effort
+    for online streams, not a network latency guarantee. Bitstream, DSD, encoded
+    output and frame-oriented devices use ordinary replacement without overlap.
+    Video switches normally; video frames are not blended. This command replaces
+    the playlist, like ``loadfile ... replace``, and accepts the same per-file
+    option pairs. It does not schedule automatic cross-fading between entries.
+
 ``seek <target> [<flags>]``
     Change the playback position. By default, seeks by a relative amount of
     seconds.
@@ -2493,6 +2522,22 @@ Property list
     envelope. Device recreation starts at the last requested target. Property
     observers update at the same frequency as ``audio-pts``.
 
+``audio-crossfade``
+    Native cross-fade state, as a map with these sub-properties:
+
+    ``active``
+        True while outgoing PCM remains or its final mixed sample is queued.
+    ``waiting``
+        True while the incoming file has not attached its audio output chain.
+    ``remaining``
+        Estimated seconds of retained output remaining, including its final
+        queued completion deadline. Pausing freezes the output clock.
+    ``available``
+        Seconds of currently buffered PCM eligible for a future overlap. This
+        excludes samples already sent to the audio hardware.
+    ``supported``
+        True when the current output supports this PCM mixer.
+
 ``audio-pts``
     Current audio playback position in current file in seconds. Unlike ``time-pos``,
     this updates more often than once per frame. This is mostly equivalent to
